# Accepted gitleaks findings, one fingerprint per line.
#
# A fingerprint pins a single finding to one commit, file, rule and line. That is deliberately
# narrower than allowlisting a path: `.env.example` must keep being scanned, because the whole
# risk it carries is somebody pasting a real credential into it, and a path allowlist is exactly
# what would hide that.
#
# Nothing here may be a live secret. Anything that turns out to be one is rotated and removed
# from history, not added to this file.

# The federation idempotency key placeholder in `.env.example`, matched by the generic-api-key
# rule on length alone. It was deliberately invalid, so copying the example file produced a
# service that refused to start rather than one running on a guessable key. HEAD uses the
# `<placeholder>` convention now, but the old value stays reachable from that commit, and history
# is not worth rewriting for a string that was never a secret.
#
# The literal itself is not quoted here: this file is scanned too, and repeating a flagged value
# in the note that excuses it simply relocates the finding.
3704369ba41abad2518e0840d662a86c3b7f44b6:.env.example:generic-api-key:52

